<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lombardy</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lombardy is a region of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>, one of the wealthiest parts of the peninsula. It came under Austrian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> rule in the eighteenth century and formed part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-lombardy-venetia">Kingdom of Lombardy-Venetia</a> after 1815. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> lost Lombardy in 1859 during <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-unification">Italian unification</a>, one of the setbacks that preceded the creation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austro-Hungarian Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lombardy.jpg" alt="Lombardy" width="320" height="403" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: TUBS · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lombardy_in_Italy.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The war of 1859 ended with defeat at Solferino and the loss of <b>Lombardy</b>, and the expensive army and an unstable currency left the state close to bankruptcy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/austrian-empire">Austrian Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>…but the defeats of the 1850s and 1860s forced a reconstruction of the imperial structure: the loss of <b>Lombardy</b> in the Second Italian War of Independence (1859), and the Austro-Prussian War of 1866, which excluded Austria from German affairs after the defeat at Königgrätz and cost it Venetia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/austro-hungarian-empire">Austro-Hungarian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Duchy of Milan grew out of the signoria, or lordship, that the Visconti family had built over Milan and the cities of <b>Lombardy</b> since the late thirteenth century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-milan">Duchy of Milan · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>At the Treaty of Campo Formio in 1797 Francis II gave up the Austrian Netherlands and <b>Lombardy</b> in return for Venice, and after defeats at Marengo and Hohenlinden in 1800 the Treaty of Lunéville of 1801 confirmed French control of the left bank of the Rhine and set…</blockquote><a href="/empire/habsburg-monarchy">Habsburg Monarchy · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In alliance with Napoleon III of France, Sardinia defeated Austria in 1859 at Magenta and Solferino and gained <b>Lombardy</b>; in 1860 plebiscites joined Tuscany, Parma, Modena and the Romagna, while Savoy and Nice were ceded to France as the price of French support.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Cavour’s diplomacy, from the Crimean War to the agreement with Napoleon III at Plombières in 1858, drew France into war with Austria and allowed the kingdom to absorb <b>Lombardy</b>, the central Italian duchies and, after Giuseppe Garibaldi’s Expedition of the Thousand, the south.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-sardinia">Kingdom of Sardinia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
